{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503553797,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEyMTkwOTk4NTQ="},"added":["!! urgent !!"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1503554854,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNDU0MjI1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324542253"},"message":"but where do you see those messages sneaking through?  the worst is whenever something does not work and hard to figure out why -- raising overall level to DEBUG makes output too cluttered to easily catch \"ah command failed to run correctly when it should have\".","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503555304,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNDU0MzQyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324543425"},"message":"Check the latest PR for an example. The error I want to act on has already\nflooded the scrrrn and will appear a total of three times.\n\n\n\nOn Aug 24, 2017 08:07, \"Yaroslav Halchenko\" \u003cnotifications@github.com\u003e\nwrote:\n\n\u003e but where do you see those messages sneaking through? the worst is\n\u003e whenever something does not work and hard to figure out why -- raising\n\u003e overall level to DEBUG makes output too cluttered to easily catch \"ah\n\u003e command failed to run correctly when it should have\".\n\u003e\n\u003e —\n\u003e You are receiving this because you authored the thread.\n\u003e Reply to this email directly, view it on GitHub\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324542253\u003e,\n\u003e or mute the thread\n\u003e \u003chttps://github.com/notifications/unsubscribe-auth/AAIVH7OPB50Pi8xZZ9IGvpdvWOwDnbGqks5sbRMmgaJpZM4PA65i\u003e\n\u003e .\n\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503566292,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNDU4MTE4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324581186"},"message":"I just need a way to be able to say \"shut up, I'll deal with this\". The only way that I have is to swallow _any_ logs. In case of #1752 the function that logs \"ERROR\" (which at least is actually an error) is just a one-liner. So I could just not use it, call `git tag` myself, and set `expect_failure=True`, in which case it would do exactly what I want. So we either have a switch general switch (major RF), move to DEBUG (minimal change), or have constant pressure to avoid using inflexible low-level code.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1503584052,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNDY0NzU5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324647592"},"message":"so you do set `expect_fail=True` (note, that not `expect_failure`) -- it shouldn't then log ERROR I believe....:\n\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.cmd import Runner; Runner()(\"ls lksjdf\")'         \n2017-08-24 10:13:20,636 [ERROR  ] stderr| ls: cannot access 'lksjdf': No such file or directory (cmd.py:352)\n2017-08-24 10:13:20,637 [ERROR  ] Failed to run 'ls lksjdf' under None. Exit code=2. out= err=ls: cannot access 'lksjdf': No such file or directory\n|  (cmd.py:296)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"\u003cstring\u003e\", line 1, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n  File \"datalad/cmd.py\", line 120, in __call__\n    return self.run(cmd, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"datalad/cmd.py\", line 297, in run\n    raise CommandError(str(cmd), msg, status, out[0], out[1])\ndatalad.support.exceptions.CommandError: CommandError: command 'ls lksjdf' failed with exitcode 2.\nFailed to run 'ls lksjdf' under None. Exit code=2. out= err=ls: cannot access 'lksjdf': No such file or directory\n\n2 10485 -\u003e1.....................................:Thu 24 Aug 2017 10:13:20 AM EDT:.\n(git)hopa:~datalad/datalad[enh-annex-file-has-content]\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.cmd import Runner; Runner()(\"ls lksjdf\", expect_fail=True)'\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"\u003cstring\u003e\", line 1, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n  File \"datalad/cmd.py\", line 120, in __call__\n    return self.run(cmd, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"datalad/cmd.py\", line 297, in run\n    raise CommandError(str(cmd), msg, status, out[0], out[1])\ndatalad.support.exceptions.CommandError: CommandError: command 'ls lksjdf' failed with exitcode 2.\nFailed to run 'ls lksjdf' under None. Exit code=2. out= err=ls: cannot access 'lksjdf': No such file or directory\n```\n\nas you can see in the 2nd case -- no ERROR log lines. So it might be up to the handler of that exception","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503637137,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNDgyNDE5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-324824196"},"message":"I know that, but this is the problem, not the solution. This switch is inaccessible from high-level code. I want to call GitRepo.tag() (which internally calls Runner()). I do not want to call Runner myself from high-level code -- what would be the purpose of low-level code if I cannot call that.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1506859227,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzMzM3MTgyOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-333371828"},"message":"Looking into this, while at logging anyway.\n\nFrom my point of view, you're both kind of right. The unexpected ERROR thing is better for noticing what's wrong and on the other hand you should be able to just use low-level code, of course.\nThe thing is, that we need to figure out, when to expect what depending on the underlying git command.\nIn that particular case, the \"right\" way is to not complain about the ERROR logging, but to complain, that `GitRepo.tag()` isn't properly implemented yet. This is just a quick careless call of `git tag` by now.\nIf a method like that doesn't fit our needs, we should point out, that this method needs attention.\nOriginally, that's the intention to have all that parameter madness for the `Runner` and that `_git_custom_command`. To be able to have a quick implementation at `*Repo` level, that can be used upstairs and thereby have the interface for `*Repo`s we need, instead of hiding low-level calls in high level code. That way we don't have to RF, but just improve implementation within the `*Repo` classes.\n\nAs I'm going through the log swalloing in PR #1870 , I'll aim to fix those methods or at least make dedicated issues to replace this one.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1506859554,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzMzM3MjEzNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-333372135"},"message":"However, it might still be desirable to have a reserved log level for the `Runner` below DEBUG to avoid multiple reporting of the same issue and preserve a way to quickly recognize log messages coming from it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1506859771,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzMzM3MjMzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1753#issuecomment-333372330"},"message":"How about level 11 (above DEBUG, below INFO) for what was ERROR level and level 9 for what was DEBUG in `Runner`?","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1507421332,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTI4MzAyODk3OQ=="},"status":2}]}